  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
  <p>If you've ever exported a video from an iPhone or a Mac, you've likely encountered a `.mov` file. But how does it compare to the ubiquitous `.mp4`? The truth is, they are closer cousins than you might think.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-mov">What is MOV?</h2>
  <p>MOV is a proprietary video container format developed by Apple in 1998 for its QuickTime framework. It was designed to hold high-quality video, audio, and text tracks for editing and playback within the Apple ecosystem.</p>
  <p>Interestingly, when the International Organization for Standardization (ISO) was looking to create the global MP4 standard, they actually used Apple's MOV format as the blueprint. That means MP4 and MOV are structurally almost identical under the hood.</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Feature Comparison</h2>
  
  <ComparisonTable 
